Z||000009||AgaricX||12-05-2001||02:46 AM||||Ya.  In my 3800 I installed 2 proms to fool the computer.  I don't have a CAT, not do I have the sensor in front of and behind the CAT.  The CPU was running in limp mode till I popped those in.  Wasn't hard.  I was just wondering if the CPU would require complete reprogamming, or just PROM replacement.<P>Definitely keep us posted though.  I'm itching to put in a N*, but won't unless its manual.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.agaric.net/fiero/AgaricX.jpg">  <BR>'85 SE 3800SC 5 Spd<BR>I WANT A FASTBACK!!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||12-05-2001||05:03 PM||||Bump.<BR>I've been in touch with the Gentleman in question. I'm working with him and the nice folks at <A HREF="http://www.diy-efi.org" TARGET=_blank>www.diy-efi.org</A>  in order to figure out how to get this PROM out to us regular folks at minimal cost.<BR>More later....||||reg||1
Z||000011||00lE||12-05-2001||05:40 PM||||I have everything needed for this.  Would need him to send me the memcal w/eprom in order to download the bin.  There would be little or no risk to the memcal or eprom, with nothing really other than slipping the memcal into an adapter required:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.thirdgen.org/newdesign/tech/images/prom/1138134.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thirdgen.org/newdesign/tech/images/prom/1138134.jpg</A> <P>Would also be willing to burn the code to prom's for those who need it.<p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Nashco||12-07-2001||03:12 AM||||I'm still waiting to hear back from Will, I'll let you know as things develop.<P>I have been in contact with the other person that I began this thread for, but things aren't too promising on that avenue.  Here's the latest, FYI, on his PROM:<P>-"I'M ON THE MISSISSIPPI GULF COAST BTWEEN NEW ORLEANS AND MOBILE.  i WILL ONLY LET THE PROM LEAVE MY POSESSION AFTER I GET THE HOLLEY 950 RUINNING."<P>(Therefore we're waiting on him to finish another project.  If I could gain access to the PROM reader and figure it out, I'd make the drive, probably a day long trip to get there and back.  More to come on that...)<P>-"I DO HAVE ONE BUG STILL.  THE KNOCK SENSOR READS 215 KNOCK COUNTS ALL THE TIME.  IT MAY BE RETARDING THE SPARK, BUT I DON'T KNOW FOR SURE.  I STILL TOOK ON AND BEAT A VIPER GTS THOUGH."<P>(This is way beyond me right now, and it sounds like some software may have been cut out or needs tweaking.  I'll *assume* he is using the proper knock sensor.)<P>-"THERE IS SOMEONE IN TEXAS WHO IS TRYING TO SCROUNGE UP SOME CHIPS TO COPY THE PROGRAM TO, BUT HE HASN'T HAD ANY LUCK YET. HE WAS THINKING OF SELLING COPIES FOR ABOUT $200 BUT I THINK THE CHIP COST MAY EAT INTO THAT TOO MUCH.  IF SOMEONE IS WILLING TO TACKLE THE KNOCK SENSOR SITIUIATION I MIGHT BE INTERESTED IN DISCUSSING."<P>(I'm sure we can find chips, most of them are fairly cheap.  If somebody knows a chip number, I will start finding suppliers.  This seems like a catch 22, IMO...I would pay 200 bucks no prob, but not for a chip without the knock sensor figured out!?  Again, I'm willing to help a lot with something like this any way I can, but I can't decipher the knock sensor issue on my own at this point.  Any ideas?<P>-"HAVE SOMEONE CONTACT ME.  I WOULD LIKE TO TALK TO THEM IN DETAIL ABOUT HOW THEY WOULD READ IT, ETC. I'M REALLY NOT INTERESTED IN MAKING MONEY OFF THIS, BUT IF I COULD RECOVER SOME OF MY ORIGINAL INVESTMENT IT WOULD HELP."<P>If somebody is interested in this, I will send you this person's email address so that you can talk to them directly.  The reading/burning of the PROM is not nearly as big of an issue as the knock sensor issue.  Again, I think he's asking a little much for something that's not fully operational.  I agree, it is very affordable, but we are looking for a no-compromises kind of swap...right?  If you think otherwise, please let me know, but this is my take on it.<P>As I said, I'm waiting to hear back from Will...his deal sounds a little more promising, but it's still in the works.  More to come...<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>P.S.  If there are typos, forgive me, it's been a very long day <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||wcapman||12-07-2001||09:18 PM||||Hi, guys.  I'm the one with the chip.  A little info and background.  The chip will only run a 95 or earlier ECM with idle speed control throttle body. That's a 95 or earlier.  Only this PROM is needed to run the program, and there is just one PROM in the 95 computer.  I "borrowed" a PROM from another friend who has built a N*/Gerag and he got his chip from H______. (No names given because I don't want to slam anyone who may have the problems worked out by now) It had no diagnostics, so you can not read anything about engine perameters.  It ran my car okay but had some drivability problems.  It would not learn idle and the PassKeyII was still in the chip.  I also had another chip from Dr. ____ which had diagnostics but also more drivability problems and PassKeyII.  <P>The chip I have has no PassKey, No TCC and no IPC data links.   I plug an old IPC into the wiring harness to read diagnostics, but there is no transfer of data.  It starts and runs everytime (if the battery is up).<P>My reason for changing out to the Holley 950 is I have a set of heads that were ported and polished by Paul Vanderley with tighter valve springs and 4 cams from Alan Johnson that I hope to put in sometime this winter. The OBDI will not support the fuel curve or timing tables for this profile.  Hope to get it up to about 365hp at 9000 rpm (Alan said it would be good for 10,500, but I don't trust that).<P>And yes, I did run a Viper GTS and beat him twice to about 55 (ran out of room).  He got me on the punch from 60 to 100 by about 3 lengths.  <P>So, who can copy this chip or read the .bin file?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Cooter||12-07-2001||09:44 PM||||IF someone could read the bin, is there an editor anywhere for it? This is sounding like a lot of fun, but what if the chip needs tweaking.||||reg||1
Z||000015||00lE||12-08-2001||12:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>It had no diagnostics, so you can not read anything about engine perameters.  It ran my car okay but had some drivability problems.  <BR>So, who can copy this chip or read the .bin file?[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sounds like someone burned the bin with an incorrect checksum, will result in a no diag data issue every time. :-)<BR>With no diagnostics data or codes, its very optimistic to even assume the chip is working correctly, as its very likely to be in a fault mode such as "limp home".<P>The knock sensor problem is most likely hardware related.  The calpak contains the knock sensor filter circutry and it is specific to knock sensor design.  IOW, if the knock sensor itself does not match the sensor that the calpak is designed for, big problems result.  So, its probably just a matter of installing the correct knock sensor, although knowing which one is correct could be a problem without cross-referencing the calpak numbers.<P>Like I said before, reading the chip binary code and saving it to a file is child's play.  Without a good hack of the binary code, there's not much that could be changed to fix or improve it. Could still be very useful to those installing a manual northstar setup.||||reg||1
Z||000016||wcapman||12-08-2001||09:42 AM||||Sure is good to find some people out there who seem to know how to address this. On the issue of the knock sensor, I never changed the hardware.  The computer is from the car that the engine came from and the knock sensor has never been changed. I had another PROM, which had drivability problems, such as the fuel injectors would cut off in the coast down mode and you had to do an electric restart every time you stopped the car.  The diagnostics said the knock sensor was registering no knocks, but when you tapped on the side of the block it would count the taps.  The shop manual says this is the way to test it.<P>I've had this chip running now for almost 3 years.  There may now be other people out there with chips also. I do undertand that cloned copies of the Aurora chip from the Shelby car were available...but fuel mapping would be way off for a stock engine.<P>I'm not sure that the knock sensor issue is a problem with my chip.  Using the diagnostics I can generate a timing map and the advance is 32deg at 5000 or so, which seems about right.  Also the IPC says that the gas is premium, and I understand that knock sensor is used to set octane.  If knock is detected it lowers the timing and the IPC reads a lower octane.<P>I have no engine knock problems that I can tell, but I always run premium.  I suspect that this chip would perform as well in almost all applications.  <P>Now, hooking up the knock sensor on the Holley 950 is my next hurdle.  There is an input for it, but Holley recommends that you use a knock sensor and external amp all designed for a specific application.  Since the amp was internal to the N* the dealer has not been able to help me on this one.  Alan said to ignore the knock sensor, and just map the timing to premium and let it go.  Anyone got any thoughts on this?<P>Also has anyone found a source of PROMs?  Superchips says they can get them but they are about $200 their cost.  ||||reg||7
Z||000017||Nashco||12-08-2001||11:20 AM||||I don't know where to get a N* chip, but if somebody knew a chip style, I could definitely search the net for one.  I know we can get chips for the stock Fiero ECM for about 3 bucks...so there's got to be something out there for under 25 bucks, I would think.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||12-08-2001||12:46 PM||||From the GMECM list at <A HREF="http://www.diy-efi.org" TARGET=_blank>www.diy-efi.org</A>  :<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><P>Subject: Re: One of a kind Northstar PROM: the saga continues<P>     1 . The NorthStar uses a 512k Eprom. That's a common . The difference is in the Image "loaded" into the prom.<BR>512k of memory space is 512k of memory space. For starters.<BR>It's "usually safe to pick "generic"  to read the prom.  Unless you know exactly which manufacturer produced the Eprom.<BR>Different manufacturers use various algorithms and programming voltages.<P>     2. The Same # ECMs share the Same components.<P>     3. The ECMs " Adaptive Memory  has nothing to do with reading or<BR>burning another image into the Eprom.<P>     4. There's usually a help file that is included with most Programming<BR>Devices. Read it carefully before you use the device.<BR>         Learn the Basics first , save yourself lots of grief.<BR>         Len<P>&gt;I've talked to the gentleman with the one of a kind PROM, and he says that<BR>&gt;it's packaged unusually (some kind of T-handle arrangement). I haven't<BR>&gt;seen it yet.<BR>&gt;In order to work with the N* ECM, it has to have the same pinout as the<BR>&gt;stock PROM, right?<BR>&gt;I which case a PROM reader should be able to read it the same way as it<BR>&gt;would a stock Caddy PROM, right? (Yes, I'm a bit of a newbie) Do all GM<BR>&gt;PROMs read the same way?<BR>&gt;Is there anything special that anyone knows about Caddy PROMs and the<BR>&gt;learning habits of Northstars that makes copying this PROM in the usual<BR>&gt;fashion not possible?<BR>&gt;Does the relationship of the NV memory used for learning bear on how a<BR>&gt;PROM is read/burned?<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt;Thanks!<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt;Will<P><BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>||||reg||1
Z||000019||00lE||12-08-2001||06:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by wcapman:<BR><B>) The chip I have has no PassKey, No TCC and no IPC data links.   I plug an old IPC into the wiring harness to read diagnostics, but there is no transfer of data.  It starts and runs everytime (if the battery is up).<P>My reason for changing out to the Holley 950 is I have a set of heads that were ported and polished by Paul Vanderley with tighter valve springs and 4 cams from Alan Johnson that I hope to put in sometime this winter. The OBDI will not support the fuel curve or timing tables for this profile.  Hope to get it up to about 365hp at 9000 rpm (Alan said it would be good for 10,500, but I don't trust that).<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I am not sure we are on the same track here. ;-)<P>wcapman, if you have no data flow from the pcm, how do you generate timing maps?  I must be misunderstanding things here, but wanted to note that if you cannot hook a scanner up to the diagnostic port to monitor sensor parameters, AND obtain trouble codes that may have been set, the prom is essentially worthless.  So we should clarify this point at least.<P>A bit on my flavor of terminolgy so that folks can understand what I am talking about with the knock sensor issues.<P>A prom is just a generic term for EPROM.  This is the chip that contains the binary data, and it is soldered directly to the CALPAK.  The calpak w/eprom soldered on, is inserted into the socket within the PCM.<BR>If you did not de-solder the factory eprom from the original calpak and then solder in the new prom, you could possibly have the incorrect ESC(knock) filter circitry for your OEM knock sensor, since the filter circuit is located on the calpak itself.  <BR>GM did not change the knock sensor from 93-94, so its very likely yours is correct, if the person who created this new chip used a 93-94 calpak.<P>Its also doubtful that you have the incorrect knock sensor if you do not have any unexplained knock counts or a code set, but the previous posts indicated that this was not the case, just another misunderstanding I guess.<P>EPROMS are cheap, can be obtained from many electronics souces such as digikey, etc. for less than $3.00  The calpak on the other hand is rather expensive ($40-$100) but can still be obtained directly from GM or even  from a salvage PCM out of a similar car.<P>BTW, if you know where the fuel and timing tables are in the code (ie.. a good code hack) you can adjust an SD system to accomodate just about any setup imaginable, including yours! MAF setups are a bit more challenging, and a very radical setup would probably require some big $$$ for an aftermarket custom MAF.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||Nashco||12-08-2001||06:43 PM||||Hmmm...slight miscommunication on my part with Will; he doesn't have the PROM chip, but does have access to it.  This chip is supposed to be without flaws....I think...<P>I was wondering, is the Caddy PROM a chip that can be erased?  If so, old PROM chips could be obtained from the junkyard, erased, then burned with the new .bin file.  This would be especially useful for somebody who's making a project out of the N*; they could take the PROM out of the computer from the donor vehicle, mail it off and get it erased/burned, then get it sent back and just plug it in.  <P>The biggest holdup, obviously, is getting the .bin from the PROM that is best (not sure if it's Will's or wcapman's yet).<P>I still haven't figured out what chip the N* uses...a lot of dead ends so far.  If I could find that out, that would make this a lot easier; if we know of a reliable and affordable chip source that would be fantastic.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Cooter||12-08-2001||07:24 PM||||I hope this works out because I'd love to have a N* in an old roadster or T-bucket. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||00lE||12-09-2001||01:23 AM||||The eprom erases with ultraviolet light (UV).<P>You can modify a standard eprom UV eraser to accept the entire calpak (no need to remove/replace the eprom).  The calpak can  then be re-burned with the new code directly by using a modified 32 pin header:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.thirdgen.org/newdesign/tech/images/prom/1138122.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thirdgen.org/newdesign/tech/images/prom/1138122.jpg</A> <P>with just about any common prom burner.<p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 12-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||Nashco||12-09-2001||02:32 AM||||Sweet!  That would make it easy to reburn old PROM chips for a swap!  I'd happily pay the couple hundred bucks plus the cost of the adapter to be able to copy, erase, and burn the chip!<P>00|E, what're you using for a reader/burner setup?  I've had people recommend xtronics.com before, looks like you have their's, am I right?  Do you happen to know what chip style the N* uses?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||24.116.114.221||reg||1
Z||000024||00lE||12-09-2001||07:07 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B><BR>00|E, what're you using for a reader/burner setup?  I've had people recommend xtronics.com before, looks like you have their's, am I right?  Do you happen to know what chip style the N* uses?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yep, the pocket programmer works flawlessly for me.  I would guess that the N* eprom itself is a 27C512, very common/cheap.<BR>The part numbers on the N* calpaks would be needed to obtain a new one from GM, and to ensure that the correct calpak was obtained from a salvage yard.<P>The PCM used in 94 was the 16197429, if that helps any...<P><BR>I doubt that anyone would be able to charge much for the prom binary code. Someone like me would just get ahold of a chip, grab the binary off it, and provide it free to anyone who wanted it ;-)<P>What is really needed is a very well done commented disassembly of the binary (code hack), so that the binary code can be optimized for performance modifications.  <BR>Given the popularity of these motors for hotrod's, I would be very suprised if such a hack did not exist somewhere.||63.224.111.160||reg||1
Z||000025||terryk||12-09-2001||02:02 PM||||The NS chip I worked on has two 27C512 eproms, not one. 92/93 4.6L L37.||||reg||1
Z||000026||wcapman||12-10-2001||07:39 PM||||Okay, let me state this one more time.  The chip I have has full diagnostics.  They are read through the climate control section of the INSTRUMENT PANEL CLUSTER. The display in a single line on the bottom of the speedo. You must have an IPC to read them.  The binary code coming out of the N* is not read by a Tech 2000, or at least it wasn't back in 1998 when I did all of this.<P>I had a technician from a chip company take my original chip and two other chips that were SUPPOSED to run the N*/manual but didn't and make a single chip where everything more or less works (ex knock sensor).  We obtained a chip from a junkyard computer to burn my current chip onto.  So from what I have read the CALPACK is built into the chip carrier.  That might explain the problem with the knock sensor, because it worked okay on the other two chips.  Again, the knock sensor is not a show stopper.  But I guess you should match the right chip with the right year.<P>This guy did this "moonlighting" and he used some company equipment so I shall not mention his name or the company.  Sorry.  But I recall that he had a heck of time trying to read the .bin code and make corrections to the chip.  To my knowledge, you can not diddle with the fuel map or timing map through the data link.  It must be written to the chip when programmed.  I took this car to a Dyno shop once hoping that we could play with it but they couldn't read a thing on the data line.  Caddy did this right!  I didn't see any point in running dyno numbers on it since they wanted $150 and we wouldn't get to change anything. <P>The chip I have has a modified timing and fuel map.  While you might want to play with it, there is more than adequate performance already there.<BR> <BR>It has been 3 years since I went through all of this.  Yes, I've been driving the car for 3 years now when I'm not working on the suspension, or something else.  During that time my friend left the chip company and I don't know how to find him. I do know that he had a hard drive crash and lost the .bin file back in 99.<P> So there we are.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Nashco||12-10-2001||08:05 PM||||I'm very interested in obtaining this .bin file.<P>I have been very busy the last week; I am moving, leaving work, travelling to the other corner of the country, and trying to sell a bunch of stuff all at the end of this week...sorry if it appears as though I've lost ambition.  I really haven't, just my ambition is being divided into too many things right now!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I will be looking around on the net trying to find a reliable, cheap source of the chip style that terryk and 00|E cited.<P>00|||24.116.114.221||reg||1
Z||000028||wcapman||12-10-2001||09:52 PM||||Also, I'm not grasping the calpak part...would this be a part that could only be used from a junkyard chip? Is it hardware or software? Sorry for the rookie question, I bet it's one of those "righty tighty lefty loosey" things in EFI! <P>I don't know about the CALPAC part either.  BUT the EPROM(s) is in a plastic housing that looks like  a t-handle.  Since the actual chip is in the top of the T I assume that other things could be below it on between the pins and the proms.  Is this wehere the CALPAC resides?<P>I just found in the JEG's catalog an ENGINE KNOCK ALERT from MSD for $143.  This may be the ticket I'm looking for for the Holley 950.  Anyone know anything about this?||||reg||1
Z||000029||00lE||12-10-2001||09:56 PM||||The "chip carrier" you are refering to IS the memcal (or calpak if you prefer).  I already have an adapter that should be able to read the binary directly from the memcal (ie.. no need to remove the eprom from the memcal).<BR>Here is what a typical GM memcal looks like:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/calpak.gif"> <P>One end contains the knock filter circuitry, and the other contains the eprom.  You can see the actual eprom through one of the small holes (the one on the left actually).<P><B>ALL</B> GM production vehicles using electronic engine managment have a diagnostics port where a scanner CAN be attached to provide real time (or close to it) aquisition of sensor information.  <P>The only real prerequisite for obtaining the eprom binary code is that it must be an OBDI system, NOT OBDII.<P>NASHCO, the memcal already has an eprom of the correct type on it.  You would need the <B>entire memcal</B> anyway, so I'd just give up on the separate chip idea. ;-)<P> The 27c512's are available just like I said, but it would require some fairly advanced soldering technique to remove the OEM eprom from the memcal and either replace it with a zif socket or solder in a new eprom.  I would only go to this extreme if there were an eeprom (i.e. electrically erasable prom) available that would work, but the only one that I know of is the ATMEL AT29C512, and its a 32 pin chip (NOT 28 pin).||||reg||1
Z||000030||Nashco||12-10-2001||10:17 PM||||OOOOOOOh, I get it!  LOL, I knew it would be a right tighty thing!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>So, you have the ability to read the entire memcal from OBDI.  We'll assume wcapman's memcal is OBDI.  So, we can read his entire memcal, including the PROM and the knock filtering chip and whatever else is in there.<P>Ok, hypothetical "memcal" building (no longer chips  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">):<BR>So, we have that...then what?  Then we have to erase the entire memcal with UV and use the reader/burner to send the new file into the memcal?  We can reprogram all of the stuff inside the memcal?  Is the knock filter reprogrammable with all of this...so if you use an ECM from a different year as your knock sensor or it's unknown, you can reprogram it to use a known working sensor?<P>I think my brain is finally clicking along here, I may just understand it all eventually!  (well, not all of it, but enough to not be such a dope)<P>So, the pocket programmer includes everything needed to read this memcal then?  Or is an adapter still needed?<P>Thanks so much for everybody's help so far, can't thank you enough.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<BR>*I swear, one more typo and I'm checking into a Betty Ford for typos...*<p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 12-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||wcapman||12-10-2001||10:38 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by 00lE:<P><BR><B>ALL</B> GM production vehicles using electronic engine managment have a diagnostics port where a scanner CAN be attached to provide real time (or close to it) aquisition of sensor information.  <P>I have a 1995 Cadillac shop manual and it shows a DTC connector.  But nowhere in the manual does it say to connect anything to it.  As I said earlier, I had borrowed a Tech 2000 in 1998 and it said in the manual "works with all GM cars to date EXCEPT Cadillac Northstar."  Maybe that has changed now.  I also wondered why the Aurora was not mentioned.  Maybe the computers are different.  There appears to be a lot of "stuff" on a Caddy data line, like IPC, TCC, CCC, all talking to each other.<P>I would really like to know if anyone has actually read the code and/or diddled with the fuel curve through the data line.  I have a friend who is struggling with an EPROM from H_______ in his N*/Getrag with no diagnostics and a fuel map that is not optimum.  If it can be read maybe he can find a dyno shop in the Seattle area that could do something.  Or if you guys ever figure out how to copy this chip we can get him a copy, too.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000032||wcapman||12-10-2001||10:45 PM||||While looking for information on the MSD Knock sensor I entered  <A HREF="http://www.msd.com" TARGET=_blank>www.msd.com</A>   and found a site for a company that make an EPROM reader.  Check that out.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000033||ZZ430||12-10-2001||11:03 PM||||I'm not sure if you all are still looking for EPROM information, but here are some links which may help out.  One is for an ATMEL EPROM which shows to be OTP.  Another is for the Microchip.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.atmel.com/atmel/acrobat/doc0015.pdf" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atmel.com/atmel/acrobat/doc0015.pdf</A>  <A HREF="http://batronix.com/pdf/27c512.pdf" TARGET=_blank>http://batronix.com/pdf/27c512.pdf</A> <P>Below are links to sites where you can get a programmer:<BR>Techtools ECONOROM II<BR>  (http://www.tech-tools.com/er2.htm)<BR>XTRONICS<BR>  (http://xtronics.com/memory/EPROM.htm)<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000034||00lE||12-11-2001||12:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by wcapman:<BR><B>I have a 1995 Cadillac shop manual and it shows a DTC connector. But nowhere in the manual does it say to connect anything to it. As I said earlier, I had borrowed a Tech 2000 in 1998 and it said in the manual "works with all GM cars to date EXCEPT Cadillac Northstar." <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Anything 95 and newer is uaually OBDII, as far as 95 OBDI and pre-95:<BR> If there IS a data connection terminal(dtc) then exactly what purpose would it have other than providing an access port for scanner hookup?? LOL <P>What do you think that GM uses to hook up to the data port?<P>If there really is no capability to monitor a northstar's engine parameters with a scanner through its data port, there is very little you could do to ensure that any changes made to the eprom were working as intended, and the code is essentially worthless as far as a starting point for performance modification.  Thats about as far as my interest extended, so I think I am out of this party.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000035||Nashco||12-11-2001||12:21 AM||||Perhaps it's that scanner in particular...???  Have you tried any other scanner; maybe go to a shop that has a recently updated Snap On scanner.  As 00|E is saying, it's hard to believe there is no port to plug a scanner into.<P>On the other hand, I've been told that you can monitor almost as much on the dash as most scanners can.  The only downfall is you can't monitor stuff with oscilliscope type functions, as well as get a data capture mode going.<P>My brain hurts...bedtime.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||24.116.114.221||reg||1
Z||000036||Will||12-11-2001||12:03 PM||||My $0.02 on several things:<P>Aurora: We've been reading the Aurora FSM, and it is much less dependent on outside systems than the Cadillac. The Aurora and N* use the same ECM; the only difference is in the calpak. For instance, if it doesn't receive a signal from TCS, the Aurora just ignores it; no codes, nothing. I don't know how it reacts to not having a IPC, but I'm going to try the Aurora calpak first in my car (Since I already have one, and they're only $15-20 new).<P>Filter cricuitry:<BR>The knock sensor filter is hardware. To my knowledge, it can not be reprogrammed short of making a new one and physically replacing it.<P>OBDI/II:<BR>To my knowledge OBDI chips can not be reprogrammed without removing the calpak from the ECM. OBDII computers can be reprogrammed through the ALDL, but the calpak can not be removed from the ECM. LT1 EDIT and LS1 EDIT work by connecting a serial to ALDL adapter between a PC and the ALDL connector in the car.<P>Scanners:<BR>We have the Auto X-ray brand scanner. I think it works with Caddys. I will verify.<BR>The Cadillac IPC is a fully functional scanner built into the automobile. It is even capable of limited data logging for trouble shooting purposes.||||reg||1
Z||000037||wcapman||12-11-2001||02:47 PM||||Thank you Will.  That was what I was trying to say and you spelled it out very well.  It was also my understanding that the OBD1 was continued in 1995 and was changed to the OBD2 in 1996.  The difference is in the processors, connectors, etc.  For a long time no one could tackle the OBD2 because it had one processor that ran everyting, and the tranny codes were hard to separate.  I think several people have since cracked it, but none with a manual that I know of.<P>Since the calpac is part of the chip I think that explains why my knock sensor is not working.  SO....the program .bin code should work on any OBD1 as long as the chip that is programmed is right for the engine knock sensor and the computer.  Mine was a junk yard chip.<P>All diagnostics can be read on the IPC.  It functions as well as a Tech 2000 did back in 1998.  You can even do data logging (snapshot) and other functions.  This is not a code reader that flashes lights, but a fully functioning single line display at the bottom of the speedo that spells out in english what is being measured and what the measurment is.  A poor mans Tech 2000. However you have limited input, and while you can temporarily set the injector pulse width of individual injectors you can not permanently change the fuel or timing map.<P>The Aurora ECM sounds like it might be a better starting ECM.  I was wondering why the Aurora wasn't lumped in with the Caddy on the Tech 2000. Still haven't heard if you can adjust the fuel map through the data port.<P>I can scan the diagnostics page from the 95 shop manual and post them somewhere if people are interested.  Just tell me how to do that.<P>Keep in mind that I did all this back in 1998.  I'm sure things have changed a lot since then and there are better ways to crack this thing than existed 3 years ago.<P>BTW  Will has been for a short ride in the car.  What were your impressions?  The chip got a problem?<P>||||reg||6
Z||000038||wcapman||12-11-2001||06:21 PM||||I just stopped by my favorite auto garage and asked if their scanner would read a N*.  It was a Snap-0n.  Lo and behold, the 1995 N* was in the computer.  I didn't have my car there nor do I have the standard DTC port, as I wired up a special plug to fit my portable IPC.  Eddie said all he could do was read codes, though.  he could not change anything on the chip.  Okay, so time is passing me by.<P>Anyway, I've got my new rear spindles fabricated.  It is time to start putting my new rear suspension together to get rid of the bump steer.||||reg||1
Z||000039||Nashco||12-11-2001||09:43 PM||||That is what I figured; I knew a better scan tool would be able to be used on your N*.  There are scan tools out there that do much more than read codes though; he could monitor functions (similar to built in stuff it sounds like) and run tests too.  With a high end scan tool he can watch line graphs, capture data, watch for strange sensor readings, etc.  My dad just bought a Snap On scanner that is supposed to be able to do all of this.  I can't recall the name of it now, but I'll take a good look at it first thing next week and let you know what I find out.<P>No scanner will be able to modify your chip, that is an ENTIRE other ball game!  The scanners were made for Joe Blow mechanic to monitor the system and diagnose problems, not adjust the engine after hot rodding <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Thanks for all of the info, I like how things are developing.  It seems we found out your knock sensor issue; now if we could only figure out how to fix it!  So, do you know the year/model of the car you got the donor chip from in the junk yard?  If so, you could get the knock sensor to match and hopefully solve that knock problem <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
